# Path-based authorization

API authorization is **path-prefix by role** (or by auth requirement). Restricted endpoints live under that prefix.

## Pattern

| Audience | Path prefix | Cognito / Spring rule |
|----------|-------------|------------------------|
| Admin | `/admin/**` | Cognito group `ADMIN` → `hasRole("ADMIN")` |
| Any logged-in user | `/private/**` | `.authenticated()` (any role) |
| User (role-only) | `/user/**` | Cognito group `USER` (when those endpoints exist) |

## Rules

- Admin-only APIs: `/admin/...` — never put admin-only routes outside `/admin/`
- Shared authenticated APIs (all roles): `/private/...` — e.g. account profile
- Future role-only APIs: same idea (`/user/...`, etc.)
- Shared/public routes stay outside these prefixes and get an explicit Security rule
- Controllers, OpenAPI paths, and `SecurityFilterChain` path matchers must stay aligned

```text
✅ /admin/classrooms
✅ /admin/classrooms/{id}/members
✅ /private/profile
✅ /user/profile (when USER-only endpoints exist)

❌ /classrooms with @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')") only
❌ /api/admin/... unless the app’s public API root is intentionally /api
```

When adding a restricted endpoint, put it under the correct prefix and update the security filter chain for that prefix — do not rely on method-level security alone as the primary authorization model.
